Monks, a certain monk commits an offense and holds the view that it is not an offense. Other monks, however, hold the view that it is an offense. Monks, if that monk who holds the view that it is not an offense knows regarding those monks who hold the view that it is an offense: 'These venerable ones are very learned, masters of the scriptures, bearers of the Dhamma, the Vinaya, and the Pātimokkha; they are wise, experienced, skillful, conscientious, scrupulous, and desirous of the three trainings. They are not such as would go astray through favoritism, malice, delusion, or fear, whether for my sake or for the sake of others. These monks, having carried out the act of suspension against me for not seeing the offense—

(1) If they perform the Uposatha not together with me, but apart from me; (Peyyāla);
(2) If they perform the Pavāraṇā not together with me, but apart from me;
(3) If they perform the formal acts of the Saṅgha (Saṅghakamma) not together with me, but apart from me;
(4) If they sit in their seats not together with me, but apart from me;
(5) If they sit at the drinking of rice-gruel not together with me, but apart from me;
(6) If they sit in the dining hall not together with me, but apart from me;
